**Goal** — Let a run override its configured publish mode from the CLI, a chat job or a chain run step without persisting config changes.

**Motivation** — nax already has the two halves of the mechanism: `workflow.publish` (`pr | push | none`) lets a run end without a PR, and `nax run --publish-only` (#2819) publishes such a run later from its record.

**Approach** — `nax run` gains `--publish <mode>`. A new module `packages/nax/src/cli/run-publish-override.ts` maps the flag to a CLI override, mirroring `run-context-overrides.ts`.

`bin/nax.ts` declares `.option("--publish <mode>", "Override workflow.publish for this run: pr, push or none")` on `nax run`, and `RunActionOptions` (`bin/run-action.ts`) gains `publish?: string`. The `nax run` action in `bin/nax.ts` calls `resolvePublishCliOverride(options.publish)` before `resolveRunProjectContext`: an `{ error }` result is printed with `console.error` and the process exits with code 1, so nothing loads or starts. `resolveRunProjectContext` sets `cliOverrides.workflow` from a non-error result. The CLI layer deep-merges last, so every other `workflow` key keeps its configured value. Before a handoff the run passes `none`: it ends `local-only` at once, the handoff pushes the branch, and koda's publish-only run does the CI wait. `--publish` combines with `--publish-only`, where it selects the publish depth of the rerun; `publishOnlyRefusal` does not refuse the combination.

The chat `run` job gains an optional `publish` field (`"pr" | "push" | "none"`), accepted by `nax-jobs__run` and by a chain `run` step (`CHAIN_STEP_FIELDS.run`). `buildJobArgv` appends `--publish <mode>` after the `--max-cost` arguments when it is set, and omits it otherwise. In `chain-argv.test.ts`, expect `run: ["feature", "profiles", "maxCost", "repo", "publish"]` and keep every other key unchanged in this story. For the [cli] AC spawn `bun bin/nax.ts` with `Bun.spawn`, a temporary project and `NAX_GLOBAL_CONFIG_DIR`, and a 30 s test timeout, following `trust.test.ts`.

**Scope** — In: CLI mapper and precedence, run input, tool schema and argv forwarding. Out: mandatory `none` on a same-feature chain run before a handoff (US-007); publish-only mark refusal (US-004); refusing a plain `nax run` or chat `run` on a marked feature.

**Interface**
```ts
/** The `workflow` block of `cliOverrides`, `undefined` when the flag was not passed, or the refusal text. */
export function resolvePublishCliOverride(
  value: string | undefined,
): { readonly workflow: { readonly publish: WorkflowPublishMode } } | { readonly error: string } | undefined;
```

**Goal** — Extract only the allowed files from runs with a result and enforce the receiving protocol's record-shape limits.

**Motivation** — The owner therefore ruled (koda design `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-17-flow-handoff-design.md`, APPROVED 2026-11-17): **hand over before publishing**, and **upload the run record with the handoff request**, because `.nax/features/<feature>/workflow/` is git-excluded and exists only on the machine that ran the chain.

**Approach** — A new module `packages/nax/src/workflow/handoff-record.ts` reads and checks what a handoff sends. `readHandoffRecordFiles` lists the run directories under the given workflow dir that hold a `result.json`. For each it adds a `result.json` entry and, when the directory also holds `ledger.md`, a `ledger.md` entry; `content` is the file's UTF-8 text and `mtimeMs` is `Math.trunc` of the file's `stat` mtime. Entries are ordered newest run first (by `result.json` mtime, the order `runDirsNewestFirst` uses), `result.json` before `ledger.md` within a run. The newest run and the tie check use the truncated integer `mtimeMs` that is sent, because koda compares those integers: two runs whose `result.json` mtimes truncate to the same largest value are a tie, even when their float mtimes differ. Every `content` must be text koda stores: a NUL character or an unpaired UTF-16 surrogate is refused. The limits are koda's (`packages/fleet-protocol/src/index.ts` in koda): `HANDOFF_MAX_RUNS` distinct run ids, and `HANDOFF_RECORD_MAX_BYTES` as the sum of the UTF-8 byte lengths of every `content`. `readHandoffRecordFiles` applies the shape rows of the Failure Handling table (no run, run count, tie, content text, byte cap) in that order. Stop at the first failing check, using the reason strings pinned below.

**Scope** — In: filesystem collection, ordering, wire mtimes, storable UTF-16 text and UTF-8 byte limits. Out: finished record and PRD validation (US-003); sending `package-branch*.diff`, `branch-review.json` or any workflow file other than `result.json` and `ledger.md`; changing the koda wire contract.

**Interface**
```ts
export interface HandoffRecordFile {
  readonly runId: string;
  readonly file: "result.json" | "ledger.md";
  readonly mtimeMs: number;
  readonly content: string;
}
export const HANDOFF_MAX_RUNS = 20;
export const HANDOFF_RECORD_MAX_BYTES = 524288;
export type HandoffRecordRead =
  | { readonly ok: true; readonly record: readonly HandoffRecordFile[]; readonly newestRunId: string }
  | { readonly ok: false; readonly reason: string };
/** True when koda stores the text: no NUL character and no unpaired UTF-16 surrogate. */
export function isStorableRecordText(text: string): boolean;
/** The files of every run that has a result.json, with the shape limits applied (US-002). */
export function readHandoffRecordFiles(featureWorkflowDir: string): Promise<HandoffRecordRead>;
```

**Goal** — Reject handoff records that do not describe a finished unpublished feature at the pushed commit or whose committed PRD does not match the branch and pending-story rules.

**Motivation** — nax already has the two halves of the mechanism: `workflow.publish` (`pr | push | none`) lets a run end without a PR, and `nax run --publish-only` (#2819) publishes such a run later from its record.

**Approach** — `collectHandoffRecord` calls `readHandoffRecordFiles` on `workflowFeatureDir(repoRoot, feature)` and, on success, applies the feature-record and PRD rows in table order. Both stop at the first failing check; the failure reasons are in the acceptance criteria.

The newest run's `result.json` must parse to an object with `schemaVersion: 1` and a `featureWorkflow` whose `step` is `"done"`, whose `outcome` is `"pushed"` or `"local-only"`, and whose `head` equals `input.head`: these are the handoff preconditions for a record koda can publish. Use `publishOnlyStart` as a reference only; it checks HEAD in a different order and does not require a known no-PR outcome. A done record with no `outcome` is not a finished feature record. The PRD is read at `input.head`, because koda reads it at the pushed head: `_handoffRecordDeps.git(["show", `${head}:${prdPath}`], repoRoot)`, where `prdPath` is `relative(repoRoot, join(featureDir(repoRoot, feature), "prd.json"))` (the `featureDir` SSOT, never a `.nax/features` literal) and `_handoffRecordDeps = { git: gitWithTimeout }` is the module's exported stub seam, the pattern `_configChangeDeps.git` uses in `workflow/config-change.ts:132`. A non-zero exit means the file is not committed at that head. The stdout must parse as a JSON object with a `userStories` array whose entries are all objects, else it is not a PRD (koda's `readHandoffPrd` skips non-object entries; nax refuses them, because `pendingStoryIds` cannot read them). Its `branchName` must equal `input.branch`, and `pendingStoryIds` of it must be empty. Diagnose a published run before a mismatched HEAD and do not fall back to an older record.

**Scope** — In: extend US-002's collector module with feature-record and committed PRD preflight. Out: record-file collection (US-002); mark and publication guards (US-004); approval/push/create (US-006); adopting an already-published draft PR or changing koda's wire contract.

**Interface**
```ts
/** readHandoffRecordFiles, then the newest feature record and the PRD at head checked against koda's rules (US-003). */
export function collectHandoffRecord(input: {
  readonly repoRoot: string;
  readonly feature: string;
  readonly head: string;
  readonly branch: string;
}): Promise<HandoffRecordRead>;
```

**Goal** — Add a newest-run sidecar and have both local publish-only entrypoints refuse a feature handed to koda.

**Motivation** — What is missing is a per-run way to say "do not publish", a chain step that sends the record, and a guard against publishing locally after the handoff.

**Approach** — The handed-off mark is a sidecar file, `workflow/<runId>/handoff.json`, beside the newest run's `result.json`. It is never uploaded (koda accepts only `result.json` and `ledger.md`) and it leaves `result.json` and its mtime unchanged. A new module `packages/nax/src/workflow/handoff-mark.ts` owns the mark.

`handoffMarkRefusal` returns `<feature> was handed off to koda (<what>) at <at>; koda publishes it. Delete <path> to publish it here instead`, where `<what>` is `flow <flowId> <url>` when `flowId` is set, `approval request <requestId>` when `requestId` is set, and `outcome unknown; check koda's flows first` when `outcomeUnknown` is set. The mark lives on the newest run only, so a later `nax run` that writes a newer `result.json` leaves the old mark behind on an older run and the feature is publishable again, as the design states.

Two consumers refuse a marked feature:

- `runInContext` (`packages/nax/src/workflow/feature.ts`): when `input.publishOnly === true` and `readNewestHandoffMark(fc.featureDir)` finds a mark, it throws `NaxError` `WORKFLOW_PUBLISH_ONLY_REFUSED` with message `[workflow] --publish-only: <handoffMarkRefusal text>`, before `publishOnlyStart` is consulted.
- `preparePublish` (`packages/nax/src/chat/jobs/jobs-launch.ts`): after the pending-story guard and before `publishedRefusal`, a found mark returns `{ error: "[chat] " + handoffMarkRefusal(...), publishedAtHead: true }`. The `publishedAtHead: true` flag routes the refusal through `stopRefusedPublishStep`, so a chain whose next step is that publish stops instead of waiting. It reads through a new `_jobsLaunchDeps.readNewestHandoffMark`.

Chat code imports the new workflow modules by relative path (`../../workflow/handoff-mark`), as `jobs-launch.ts` imports `../../workflow/publish-only`; the `src/workflow` barrel is unchanged. Add guard tests in the existing feature and chat launch suites, including the same-feature/same-repo chain stop.

**Scope** — In: define, read, write and format the sidecar; refuse CLI publish-only and chat publish. Out: selecting when to write the mark in a send (US-006); refusing a plain `nax run` or a chat `run` launch on a handed-off feature; clearing or listing marks with a command.

**Interface**
```ts
export const HANDOFF_MARK_FILE = "handoff.json";
export interface HandoffMark {
  readonly schemaVersion: 1;
  readonly at: string;
  /** Set when koda created the flow. */
  readonly flowId?: string;
  readonly url?: string;
  /** Set when koda held the create for approval. */
  readonly requestId?: string;
  /** Set when the branch was pushed but koda's answer never arrived (timeout, unreachable, cancelled during the create). */
  readonly outcomeUnknown?: true;
}
export function writeHandoffMark(runDir: string, mark: HandoffMark): Promise<void>;
/** The mark on the feature's newest run (by result.json mtime), or null when that run has none or it does not parse. */
export function readNewestHandoffMark(featureWorkflowDir: string): Promise<{ readonly mark: HandoffMark; readonly path: string } | null>;
/** One sentence naming the flow or request and the mark's path, used by every refusal. */
export function handoffMarkRefusal(feature: string, found: { readonly mark: HandoffMark; readonly path: string }): string;
```

**Goal** — Define, advertise and validate a single-feature handoff body alongside the existing spec form.

**Motivation** — The owner therefore ruled (koda design `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-17-flow-handoff-design.md`, APPROVED 2026-11-17): **hand over before publishing**, and **upload the run record with the handoff request**, because `.nax/features/<feature>/workflow/` is git-excluded and exists only on the machine that ran the chain.

**Approach** — The tool input takes either `features` (today's spec form, unchanged) or `handoff`, never both:

```json
{ "handoff": { "feature": "chat-layout-overflow" }, "roles": { "reviewer": "acp:claude", "supervisor": "acp:claude" }, "costCapUsd": 5 }
```

`FLOW_SEND_INPUT_SCHEMA` gains a `handoff` property (an object with one required string `feature`) and drops `features` from `required`; the runtime parser enforces exactly one of the two. A new module `packages/nax/src/chat/flow/flow-handoff-input.ts` exports the interface and functions below.

`parseFlowHandoffInput` applies `FLOW_FEATURE_NAME_RE` to `feature` and reuses the role and cost-cap checks `parseFlowSendInput` applies today (moved into shared helpers, not copied); its allowed top-level keys are `handoff`, `roles`, `costCapUsd`, and `handoff` allows only `feature`. `TOP_LEVEL_KEYS` in `flow-input.ts` gains `handoff`, so the key never reads as unknown. `parseFlowSendInput` returns its existing errors for a spec-form body and refuses a body with both keys or neither with the error `exactly one of features or handoff is required`.

`handoffStepArgv` returns `["nax-flow__send", "--handoff", feature, "--reviewer", reviewer, "--supervisor", supervisor, "--cost-cap", String(costCapUsd)]`.

`buildFlowInstructions` (`packages/nax/src/prompts/builders/flow-builder.ts`) gains one line: For a feature already run in this chat that should be published and finished by koda, call `nax-flow__send` with `handoff: { feature }` instead of `features`; its run must have ended without a PR (`publish: "none"`). Update the existing input/provider schema tests for the exact-one error and `required: ["roles", "costCapUsd"]`, preserving the rest of the spec form expectations.

**Scope** — In: runtime input validation, advertised schema, shared argument matching tokens and flow prompt line. Out: tool dispatch and sending (US-006), proposal parsing (US-007), chain admission (US-008).

**Interface**
```ts
export interface FlowHandoffInput {
  readonly feature: string;
  readonly roles: FlowSendRoles;
  readonly costCapUsd: number;
}
export function parseFlowHandoffInput(raw: Record<string, unknown>): FlowHandoffInput | { readonly errors: string[] };
/** The token list a chain step and the tool call are compared by. */
export function handoffStepArgv(input: FlowHandoffInput): readonly string[];
```

**Goal** — Validate and display a final handoff step before approving a chain, preventing same-feature runs or publishes that would invalidate the transfer.

**Motivation** — The 2026-11-04 ruling splits the work: koda owns the always-on flow, including the finish (rulings, then ready); a chat chain is the tool for one feature watched in one sitting.

**Approach** — `ChainStep.kind` widens to `ChainStepKind = ChatJobKind | "handoff"`. A handoff step's input is `{ feature, roles, costCapUsd }`. `parseStep` (`chain-tools.ts`) branches on `kind === "handoff"` before `parseChainStepInput`: it parses the input with `parseFlowHandoffInput({ handoff: { feature }, roles, costCapUsd })`, accepts no `repo` and no `checkBefore`, and skips `resolveStepTarget` (a handoff always runs in the chat's repo root); its `argv` is `handoffStepArgv(input)` and its `target` is the feature. The card line reads reviewer, supervisor and cap back from the argv tokens that follow `--reviewer`, `--supervisor` and `--cost-cap`. `resolveChainProposal` refuses a proposal, naming the step, when:

- it has a handoff step and the jobs provider has no koda connection (`JobsProviderOptions.handoffAvailable` is not true): `handoff needs a koda connection (memory.koda in the nax config)`;
- a handoff step is not the last step: `a handoff must be the chain's last step`;
- an earlier `run` step of the same feature does not set `publish: "none"`: `step <M> runs <feature> without publish "none", so it would publish its own PR before the handoff`;
- an earlier `publish` step of the same feature exists: `step <M> publishes <feature>, so there would be nothing to hand off`;
- an earlier `run` step of the same feature resolves to a repo root other than the chat's: `the handoff runs in <chat root>, but step <M> runs <feature> in <other root>`.

The chain card's step line for a handoff is `<n>. hand <feature> to koda (reviewer <r>, supervisor <s>, cost cap $<cap>) — starts after: <condition>`, and the card adds the line `koda asks for its own approval before anything billed starts`. `CHAIN_STEP_FIELDS` gains `handoff: ["feature", "roles", "costCapUsd"]`; update its pinned expectation in `chain-argv.test.ts`. Keep `ChatJobKind` and process job input parsing limited to actual jobs. Widen `ChainSummary.steps[].kind` to `ChainStepKind` here so the controller can summarize the new step type without changing its runtime admission behavior yet. The `handoffAvailable` option is added here; the host supplies it in US-008.

**Scope** — In: handoff proposal parsing, refusals, availability option and approval card rendering. Out: controller admission, step outcome and host wiring (US-008); counting the koda flow's `costCapUsd` against the chain's own `costCap`.

**Interface**
```ts
type ChainStepKind = ChatJobKind | "handoff";
```
`ChainStep.kind` uses `ChainStepKind`; `JobsProviderOptions` gains `handoffAvailable?: boolean`.
